EXT. RAILWAY BRIDGE OVER A DRY RIVER, THE BORDER - DAWN

A steel truss bridge, half-collapsed, spanning a river that has been dry since the drought. Sand and cracked clay below. Ahead, a checkpoint of sandbags, flags drooping, a rusted jeep.

Three soldiers lie prone on the east approach, covered by a torn tarp. SERGEANT KASIMIR OYELARAN-TREMBLAY (40s, desert scarf, sunburned neck) peers through binoculars. Next to him, CORPORAL INÉS VARGAS-DUBOIS (20s, a long rifle, a patient eye) sights down her scope. Behind them, PRIVATE JABARI NKRUMAH-LEIGH (19, radioman, pack heavy with coiled wire) sweats.

KASIMIR
Four at the checkpoint. Two on the jeep.

INÉS
Three on the jeep.

KASIMIR
Show me.

INÉS
Left side. Under the canopy.

KASIMIR
(adjusting)
Three.

JABARI
Is that good or bad, Sergeant?

KASIMIR
It's three.

The sun rises over the far bank, turning the dry river the color of a bruise. A flag stirs. Nothing else does.

KASIMIR (CONT'D)
Jabari, get the wire out.

JABARI
Sergeant, the wire's for the radio.

KASIMIR
It's for the bridge.

JABARI
I'm sorry?

KASIMIR
We're not talking to anyone, son. We're taking out the bridge.

INÉS
(softly)
That's not what the briefing said.

KASIMIR
The briefing's three days old.

INÉS
It said observe.

KASIMIR
Observe, report. And if the enemy advances in force, deny the crossing.

INÉS
There's no force. There's seven men and a jeep.

KASIMIR
Seven now.

He lowers the binoculars. A pale line of dust is rising from the horizon beyond the checkpoint, thin and straight.

JABARI
What's that?

KASIMIR
Armor.

INÉS
How many?

KASIMIR
Enough.

Jabari begins to unspool the wire, hands shaking. Inés shifts her rifle and watches the dust. Kasimir drags a pack beside him and opens it. Inside, two bricks of plastic explosive, a blasting cap, a roll of tape.

JABARI
Is the bridge empty?

KASIMIR
It is.

JABARI
Are you sure?

KASIMIR
Check the girders.

Jabari glances at Inés. She nods. He slithers out from under the tarp and crawls along the rail toward the first abutment, wire trailing behind him in the sand. Kasimir watches him go. Inés keeps her eye on the checkpoint.

INÉS
(whispering)
Sergeant, there are people on the jeep.

KASIMIR
Soldiers.

INÉS
There's a child.

KASIMIR
Where?

INÉS
On the back seat. Under the tarp.

Kasimir takes the binoculars back up. Adjusts. His jaw tightens.

KASIMIR
(muttering)
Damn it.

INÉS
Sergeant?

KASIMIR
Hold your fire, Corporal.

INÉS
I wasn't going to fire.

KASIMIR
Then hold the child.

INÉS
What?

KASIMIR
Hold her in your sights. If the armor reaches the checkpoint and she's still there, we don't blow the bridge. We wait.

INÉS
How long?

KASIMIR
Until she leaves.

Out on the bridge, Jabari has reached the abutment. He glances back, and Kasimir raises a flat hand: stop. Jabari freezes. The wire lies slack across the sand between them.

The dust cloud grows. A low rumble now. The flags begin to tremble.

INÉS
(very quietly)
They're not going to wait for her.

KASIMIR
No.

He puts the binoculars down. Reaches into the pack. Picks up the blasting cap and turns it over, then sets it back in the foam.

KASIMIR (CONT'D)
Jabari. Come back.

Jabari scrambles back along the rail, grabbing the wire as he goes. The rumble becomes engines. A line of tanks emerges from the dust, small and steady, rolling toward the bridge.

Inés lowers her rifle. Kasimir gathers the pack. The three of them slide back down the embankment and lie flat under the tarp as the first tank rolls onto the span, and the bridge groans, and holds.
